  "name": "Question 10",
  "problem": "Find the **polar coordinates** of the point where the polar curve $r=\\frac{13}{2}$ intersects the negative $y$-axis. Express the polar angle in radians.\n\nThe point of intersection in polar coordinates is $(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$",
  "worked_solution": "The polar curve $r=\\frac{13}{2}$ is a circle of radius $\\frac{13}{2}$ centered at the origin. It crosses the negative $y$-axis where $r=\\frac{13}{2}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039331/assets/2399108905ce184b2b2d2d299f5e52539af01228b484316ece8b2efdd6a3b852.png)\n\nTherefore, the point of intersection in polar coordinates is $(\\,\\frac{13}{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\,).$",
